          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "67af2ad7-0956-3442-af7e-9e58d50d4b9f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C and C++ is used to dynamically allocate memory. The vulnerability arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or calculated, leading to buffer overflow, integer overflow, or other memory corruption issues. In the given code snippet, `malloc(Num_Warps_per_Grid * sizeof(int))`, if the `Num_Warps_per_Grid` variable is controlled by the user or an external input, it can lead to a potential vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate and sanitize the input that is used to determine the size of the memory to be allocated.\n2. Use functions that limit the amount of memory allocated, such as `calloc`, which initializes the allocated memory to zero.\n3. Consider using modern C++ features like smart pointers (`std::unique_ptr`, `std::shared_ptr`) which provide automatic, deterministic memory management.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\nif (Num_Warps_per_Grid > 0 && Num_Warps_per_Grid <= MAX_SAFE_NUM) {\n    int* ptr = (int*) malloc(Num_Warps_per_Grid * sizeof(int));\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle malloc failure (e.g., by returning from the function).\n    }\n    // Use ptr...\n    free(ptr);\n} else {\n    // Handle invalid Num_Warps_per_Grid value.\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `MAX_SAFE_NUM` is a constant that you should define in your program to be the maximum safe number of `int`s that you can allocate.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example does not require any additional library dependencies beyond the standard C++ libraries.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
