              "id": "68ce4e97-e548-3524-8a99-141e4f6244cd",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. The size of this block is specified in bytes. The function returns a pointer to the first byte of the allocated block. If the function fails to allocate the requested block of memory, a null pointer is returned.\n\nThe vulnerability in this case could be due to the lack of error checking after the `malloc()` call. If `malloc()` fails to allocate the requested memory and returns a null pointer, and the program continues to use this null pointer, it could lead to undefined behavior, crashes, or security vulnerabilities such as null pointer dereferences.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAlways check the return value of `malloc()`. If it returns a null pointer, this means that the memory allocation has failed. The program should handle this error appropriately, for example by cleaning up and terminating, or by trying to allocate a smaller amount of memory.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```c++\nunsigned long long int *ptr = (unsigned long long int*) malloc(NUM * sizeof(unsigned long long int));\nif (ptr == NULL) {\n    // handle error, for example by terminating the program\n    fprintf(stderr, \"Memory allocation failed!\\n\");\n    exit(1);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerability Guide](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)"
