              "id": "718c52f7-4541-3b50-88c9-2ea2e0b19966",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. The size of this block is specified in bytes. The function returns a pointer to the first byte of the allocated block. If the function fails to allocate the requested block of memory, a null pointer is returned.\n\nThe vulnerability in this case could be due to the lack of error checking after the `malloc()` call. If `malloc()` fails to allocate the requested memory and returns a null pointer, and the program continues to use this null pointer, it could lead to undefined behavior, crashes, or security vulnerabilities such as null pointer dereferences.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAlways check the return value of `malloc()`. If it returns a null pointer, this means that the memory allocation has failed. The program should handle this error appropriately, for example by cleaning up and terminating, or by trying to allocate a smaller amount of memory.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```c++\nunsigned long long int *ptr = (unsigned long long int*) malloc(NUM * sizeof(unsigned long long int));\nif (ptr == NULL) {\n    // handle error, for example by terminating the program\n    fprintf(stderr, \"Memory allocation failed!\\n\");\n    exit(1);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerability Guide](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "731077a0-f0f8-3510-903c-cf709fee8efe",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. The size of this block is specified in bytes. The function returns a pointer to the first byte of the allocated block. If the function fails to allocate the requested block of memory, a null pointer is returned.\n\nThe vulnerability in this case could be due to the lack of error checking after the `malloc()` call. If `malloc()` fails to allocate the requested memory and returns a null pointer, and the program continues to use this null pointer, it could lead to undefined behavior, crashes, or security vulnerabilities such as null pointer dereferences.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAlways check the return value of `malloc()`. If it returns a null pointer, this means that the memory allocation has failed. The program should handle this error appropriately, for example by cleaning up and terminating, or by trying to allocate a smaller amount of memory.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```c++\nunsigned long long int *ptr = (unsigned long long int*) malloc(NUM * sizeof(unsigned long long int));\nif (ptr == NULL) {\n    // handle error, for example by terminating the program\n    fprintf(stderr, \"Memory allocation failed!\\n\");\n    exit(1);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerability Guide](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "72565f51-0c16-34cd-bdc7-725a2584e1e0",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. If the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated, it can lead to several vulnerabilities such as buffer overflow, memory corruption, or denial of service. This is because an attacker can provide a large value for `memsize` that can cause the program to allocate more memory than is available, leading to a crash or other unexpected behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate the size of the memory to be allocated. This includes checking that the size is not negative and that it does not exceed the maximum allowable size. You should also handle the case where `malloc()` fails to allocate the requested memory, which returns a null pointer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the `malloc(memsize)` vulnerability:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nif (memsize <= 0 || memsize > MAX_SIZE) {\n    // Handle error: invalid size\n} else {\n    void* ptr = malloc(memsize);\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle error: memory allocation failed\n    } else {\n        // Use allocated memory\n        free(ptr);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `MAX_SIZE` should be replaced with the maximum size of memory that your program should allocate.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the C standard library, so you need to include `stdlib.h` to use it.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            }